message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Engineering Political Schelling Points: Disclosure Removes Coordination Only When Commonly Known'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/engineering-political-schelling-points.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-engineering-political-schelling-points
abstract: When many accounts amplify a call to collective action, the message matters as evidence that the action can succeed and as a shared reference for predicting one another. A regime-change global game separates the two, with the message's perceived precision set by beliefs about its sources and a commonly believed reach distinct from actual receipt. The equilibrium cutoff decomposes exactly into a closed-form evidential channel and a coordination channel whose marginal size is the evidential response times $1/(1-\kappa v)$; at baseline coordination is 80% of the response when receipt is common knowledge. Each receiver's response depends on commonly believed receipt alone. A message every receiver believes uninformative has no effect however visible, so publicity multiplies evidence and never selects among outcomes as a pure focal point would. Disclosing that 3 accounts share one source removes 11% of their effect on receivers' participation at full reach for an isolated receiver, 71% when the disclosure is common knowledge, and intermediate amounts for labels; a public disclosure that also makes receipt common knowledge amplifies the message, to first order, exactly when a publicity-credibility ratio exceeds one. With erosion of the status quo, temporary messages persist in the noise-free limit above a closed-form feedback threshold, but under belief-consistent shocks only where shocks are small relative to erosion. A laboratory design, including a cell that varies only public acknowledgment of receipt, needs 7 to 81 groups of 20 per cell. The analysis covers campaigns perceived as at most 8 independent reports at baseline.
